  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, Mr. ___. I’m Dr. ___, the neurology team. I understand you came in with some dizziness and trouble finding words earlier today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, that’s right. I woke up at 4:30 this morning feeling like the room was spinning. Objects seemed to be moving. I’ve never felt quite like that before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. And you mentioned some word-finding difficulty when you were in the ER?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes. When I was trying to tell the nurse my medical history, I just couldn’t pull up the words. It scared me. But it got better after a few minutes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s good it improved. The fact that it came on suddenly and you have a history of TIAs makes us want to be very careful. We’ve already done a CT scan of your head to rule out a stroke."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had four TIAs before, all about a year ago. But this feels different. More like vertigo."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re right. The spinning sensation, worse when you move your head or sit up, is classic for vertigo. But because of your history, we need to be sure it’s not a new stroke."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:28:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Makes sense. I’ve been feeling weak and tired for about a week. Could that be related?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:29:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Possibly. That could suggest a viral illness. Sometimes a virus can affect the inner ear and cause vertigo—what we call viral vestibulopathy."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So not a stroke?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The CT scan came back normal—no bleeding or stroke. But we’ll do an MRI later today for a more detailed look. We also did a chest X-ray since you have a pacemaker, to make sure it’s safe for the MRI."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, right. My pacemaker is MRI-incompatible. But I walked across the street fine earlier. I’m still a bit unsteady, though."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:33:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, and your Romberg test showed some sway, which fits with vertigo. We’ll keep you lying down for now and avoid sudden movements."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:34:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about my medications? I take apixaban for atrial fibrillation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:35:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re continuing your apixaban. Given your history, we also started aspirin temporarily until we rule out stroke. Once the MRI is clear, we’ll stop the aspirin."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my blood pressure meds—amlodipine and clonidine?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:37:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve held those for now. Your blood pressure is stable, and we don’t want to drop it too low if your symptoms are related to blood flow issues."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:38:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. And my testosterone shot? I usually get it weekly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:39:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re holding that for now. Since you’ve been feeling unwell, it’s safer to delay it until you’re more stable. You’ll follow up with endocrinology."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Fair enough. What’s next?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:41:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re scheduling an MRI of your brain and neck with contrast. We also ordered one of your cervical spine and pituitary, just to be thorough."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:42:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Pituitary? Because of the vision changes?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:43:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. You mentioned spatial disorientation and past vision issues. We want to rule out any mass or structural cause."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:44:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my mitochondrial disease—could that be playing a role?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Absolutely. Your body doesn’t handle stress well—like infections or metabolic changes. This could be a viral trigger on top of your autonomic dysfunction."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:46:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had worse tinnitus lately too. And my hearing’s always been worse on the right."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2191-08-15 17:47:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We noted that. The vertigo could be related to your inner ear. The MRI will help assess the vestibular nerves."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2191-08-16 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. I’ve reviewed your MRI results. The good news is: no stroke, no bleed, no tumor. Your brain and blood vessels look stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2191-08-16 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. So what is it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2191-08-16 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Most likely viral vestibulopathy. The fact that you felt unwell a week before, plus the vertigo and no other neurological deficits, points to that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2191-08-16 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the word-finding issue?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2191-08-16 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Could have been a brief episode of brain fog from the vertigo or mild cerebral hypoperfusion. But no stroke signs. Your exam today shows only mild difficulty with word recall—much better than yesterday."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2191-08-16 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about the spine MRI? I have a lot of neck arthritis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2191-08-16 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, you have degenerative changes, especially at C4-C5, but no spinal cord compression or new lesions. It’s stable compared to your last scan."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2191-08-16 09:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the pituitary?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2191-08-16 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Normal. No mass or abnormality. So we can rule that out as a cause."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2191-08-16 09:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So I can go home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2191-08-16 09:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. We’ll restart your blood pressure meds—amlodipine and clonidine. Continue apixaban. And you can resume your testosterone shot with your endocrinologist."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2191-08-16 09:11:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Any restrictions?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2191-08-16 09:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take it easy for a few days. Avoid sudden head movements. If the dizziness worsens or you develop new symptoms—like weakness, vision loss, or slurred speech—come back immediately."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2191-08-16 09:13:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thank you, Doctor."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2191-08-16 09:14:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll send a note to your primary doctor and neurologist. Follow up with them in a week."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2191-08-16 17:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just a final check before discharge. How are you feeling?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2191-08-16 17:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Much better. The room isn’t spinning as much. I walked down the hall without help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2191-08-16 17:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Excellent. Your gait is steady, and your coordination has improved. We’re comfortable sending you home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2191-08-16 17:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll keep taking all my meds as before?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2191-08-16 17:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, exactly as prescribed. And stay hydrated—important with your mitochondrial condition."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2191-08-16 17:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will do. Thanks again for taking care of me."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2191-08-16 17:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Of course. Take care, and we’ll see you in follow-up if needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2191-08-16 17:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "One last thing—should I be worried about another TIA?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2191-08-16 17:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your risk factors are well-managed—on apixaban, blood pressure controlled. This episode doesn’t look like a TIA, but always be alert to new symptoms. You’re doing well."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2191-08-15 17:21:00",
    "admission_end": "2191-08-16 18:08:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A male patient with a complex medical history including mitochondrial disease, atrial fibrillation, prior TIAs, dysautonomia, and coronary artery disease was admitted for acute onset vertigo and transient word-finding difficulty. Initial concern for stroke prompted a CT head and subsequent MRI/MRA of the brain and neck, all of which were negative for acute infarct, hemorrhage, or vascular abnormality. The patient reported feeling unwell for a week prior, and given the clinical course and absence of stroke findings, the symptoms were attributed to viral vestibulopathy, possibly compounded by autonomic dysfunction. His home apixaban was continued, aspirin was briefly added and then discontinued, and antihypertensives were held initially but resumed at discharge. The patient improved clinically, with resolution of vertigo and return to baseline cognition. He was discharged home in stable condition with instructions to resume all prior medications and follow up with his neurology and endocrinology teams.",
    "problems": [
      "Viral infection, unspecified",
      "Dizziness and giddiness",
      "Mitochondrial metabolism disorder, unspecified",
      "Familial dysautonomia [Riley-Day]",
      "Atherosclerotic heart disease of native coronary artery without angina pectoris",
      "Presence of coronary angioplasty implant and graft",
      "Old myocardial infarction",
      "Essential (primary) hypertension",
      "Unspecified atrial fibrillation",
      "Personal history of other venous thrombosis and embolism",
      "Personal history of transient ischemic attack (TIA), and cerebral infarction without residual deficits",
      "Long term (current) use of antithrombotics/antiplatelets",
      "Encounter for adjustment and management of other part of cardiac pacemaker"
    ]
  }
}
